
; =====================================================================
; WS2812 / NeoPixel LED DRIVER - Raspberry Pi Pico PIO
; =====================================================================
;
; PURPOSE
; -------
; This program sends data to WS2812 RGB LEDs using the Raspberry Pi
; Pico's Programmable I/O (PIO) hardware.
;
; PIO is a small, independent processor inside the RP2040 chip.
; It executes simple instructions to control GPIO pins with very
; precise timing, without the main CPU having to toggle pins manually.
;
; WS2812 LEDs use a single data wire. Each LED receives 24 bits:
;
;     8 bits GREEN, 8 bits RED, 8 bits BLUE (GRB order).
;
; Each data bit is represented by a HIGH pulse followed by a LOW pulse.
; The duration of the HIGH pulse determines whether the bit is 0 or 1.
;
;     Bit 0: Short HIGH pulse, long LOW pulse.
;     Bit 1: Long HIGH pulse, short LOW pulse.
;
; The LEDs expect approximately 800,000 bits per second (800 kHz).
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; PIO CLOCK TIMING
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; This program assumes the PIO state machine is configured to run at 8 MHz.
;
;     8 MHz = 8,000,000 PIO clock cycles per second.
;
; Therefore:
;
;     1 PIO clock cycle = 1 / 8,000,000 seconds
;                       = 125 nanoseconds (ns).
;
; Each WS2812 data bit takes 10 PIO clock cycles:
;
;     10 x 125 ns = 1250 ns = 1.25 microseconds.
;
;     1 / 1.25 us = 800,000 bits per second.
;
; The timing is divided into three sections:
;
;     T1 = 2 cycles
;     T2 = 5 cycles
;     T3 = 3 cycles
;
;     Total = T1 + T2 + T3 = 10 cycles.
;
; The resulting waveforms are:
;
;     LOGICAL 0:
;         HIGH = 2 cycles =  250 ns
;         LOW  = 8 cycles = 1000 ns
;
;     LOGICAL 1:
;         HIGH = 7 cycles =  875 ns
;         LOW  = 3 cycles =  375 ns
;
; Both bits take exactly 10 cycles (1.25 us).
;
; NOTE: These timings assume the PIO is not stalled waiting for data.
;
; =====================================================================


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; DEFINE THE PIO PROGRAM NAME
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

.program ws2812

; ".program" is an assembler directive, not a PIO instruction.
;
; It gives this program the name "ws2812".
;
; The name is used by the host program to identify and load the
; assembled instructions into a PIO state machine.
;
; An assembler directive is processed when the code is assembled.
; It is NOT executed by the PIO hardware and takes no clock cycles.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; ENABLE SIDE-SET OUTPUT
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

.side_set 1

; Side-set is a PIO feature that allows an instruction to change
; GPIO output pins AT THE SAME TIME as performing its main operation.
;
; For example:
;
;     out x, 1    side 0
;
; This single instruction does TWO things simultaneously:
;
;     1. "out x, 1" moves one data bit into register X.
;     2. "side 0" drives the side-set GPIO pin LOW.
;
; Another example:
;
;     jmp !x, do_zero    side 1
;
; This instruction:
;
;     1. Checks whether X is zero and jumps if it is.
;     2. Drives the side-set GPIO pin HIGH.
;
; WHY USE SIDE-SET?
;
; Without side-set, separate instructions would normally be needed
; to change the GPIO and perform the data-processing operation.
;
; Side-set performs both actions in the same instruction, making
; precise pulse timing much easier.
;
; WHAT DOES ".side_set 1" MEAN?
;
; The number 1 specifies that ONE bit of each PIO instruction is
; reserved for side-set output.
;
; It does NOT mean "set the pin HIGH".
;
;     .side_set 1  = Use one side-set bit (one output pin).
;     side 0       = Drive that output pin LOW.
;     side 1       = Drive that output pin HIGH.
;
; For comparison:
;
;     .side_set 2  = Use two side-set bits (two output pins).
;
; In that case, side-set values can control both pins together.
;
; IMPORTANT:
;
; This directive does not select the physical GPIO number.
;
; The host C/C++ or MicroPython program must configure:
;
;     - Which GPIO is the side-set output.
;     - The GPIO direction (output).
;     - The PIO state machine clock speed.
;
; Because this program uses mandatory side-set (no "opt" option),
; every executable instruction must specify a "side" value.
;
; Side-set also uses bits from the instruction encoding that could
; otherwise be used for delays.
;
; With ".side_set 1", four bits remain for the delay field,
; allowing 0 to 15 additional delay cycles.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; DEFINE TIMING CONSTANTS
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

.define public T1 2
.define public T2 5
.define public T3 3

; ".define" creates a named constant.
;
; A constant is a name representing a fixed numerical value.
; The assembler substitutes the value when assembling the program.
;
; For example:
;
;     T3 = 3
;
; Therefore:
;
;     [T3 - 1]
;
; is equivalent to:
;
;     [2]
;
; WHY USE NAMES INSTEAD OF NUMBERS?
;
; Names make the timing easier to understand and adjust.
;
; Instead of writing:
;
;     out x, 1 side 0 [2]
;
; we write:
;
;     out x, 1 side 0 [T3 - 1]
;
; This shows that the delay belongs to timing section T3.
;
; "public" makes these constants available to generated host-language
; headers when using the Pico SDK's PIO assembler (pioasm).
;
; These constants are not PIO registers or special hardware timers.
; They are simply names chosen by the programmer.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; IMPORTANT: WHY DO WE SUBTRACT 1 IN [T3 - 1]?
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Every PIO instruction takes ONE clock cycle by default.
;
; The number inside square brackets [] specifies how many EXTRA
; clock cycles to wait after the instruction's normal cycle.
;
; Therefore:
;
;     Total cycles = 1 instruction cycle + delay cycles.
;
; Examples:
;
;     Instruction             Total cycles
;     ------------------------------------
;     nop                     1
;     nop [0]                 1
;     nop [1]                 2
;     nop [2]                 3
;     nop [3]                 4
;
; If we want an instruction to take exactly T3 = 3 cycles:
;
;     [T3 - 1] = [3 - 1] = [2]
;
; The instruction takes:
;
;     1 normal cycle + 2 extra cycles = 3 cycles.
;
; If we wrote [T3] instead, it would take FOUR cycles.
;
; The "- 1" compensates for the instruction's built-in clock cycle.
;
; This is why all three timing sections use [Tn - 1].


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; MARK THE START OF THE AUTOMATIC REPEAT SECTION
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

.wrap_target

; PIO programs can automatically repeat a section of instructions.
;
; ".wrap_target" marks the instruction to return to when the
; state machine reaches the end of the wrap section.
;
; ".wrap" (at the bottom of this program) marks the final instruction.
;
; When execution finishes that instruction, the PIO automatically
; returns to the instruction marked by ".wrap_target".
;
; WHY IS THIS USEFUL?
;
; Normally a processor needs a JMP instruction to return to the
; beginning of a loop.
;
; PIO automatic wrapping avoids that extra instruction.
;
; This saves program memory and avoids adding another clock cycle.
;
; ".wrap_target" is an assembler directive, not an instruction.
; It does not consume any PIO clock cycles.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; LABEL: START OF THE BIT-PROCESSING LOOP
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

bitloop:

; "bitloop" is a label identifying the next instruction's address.
;
; Labels allow JMP instructions to refer to locations by name.
;
; For example:
;
;     jmp bitloop
;
; means "continue execution at the instruction labelled bitloop".
;
; A label is not an executable instruction.
; It does not consume any clock cycles.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; INSTRUCTION 1: READ THE NEXT DATA BIT AND SET GPIO LOW
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

    out x, 1        side 0 [T3 - 1]

; This instruction performs three functions:
;
;     1. Extract the next bit of LED data.
;     2. Drive the side-set GPIO LOW.
;     3. Maintain that LOW output for 3 clock cycles.
;
; BREAKDOWN:
;
; "out"
;     OUT is a PIO instruction that transfers bits from the
;     Output Shift Register (OSR) to a specified destination.
;
; "x"
;     X is one of the PIO state machine's two scratch registers.
;     The other is called Y.
;
;     Both X and Y are 32-bit registers.
;
;     In this program, X holds the current bit being transmitted:
;
;         X = 0  means logical ZERO.
;         X = 1  means logical ONE.
;
; "1"
;     Transfer exactly ONE bit from the OSR into X.
;
; WHAT IS THE OSR?
;
; The Output Shift Register is a 32-bit register inside the PIO.
; It holds data supplied by the main CPU or DMA through the TX FIFO.
;
; The OUT instruction extracts bits from the OSR.
;
; For this program, the host must configure the shift direction
; so the most significant bit is transmitted first (MSB-first).
;
; The host normally also enables automatic FIFO pulling (autopull)
; so the OSR is refilled as data is transmitted.
;
; "side 0"
;     Drive the configured side-set GPIO pin LOW.
;
;     This happens as part of the same instruction as OUT.
;
; "[T3 - 1]"
;     T3 = 3.
;
;     The instruction normally takes 1 clock cycle.
;     The delay adds another 2 cycles.
;
;     Total = 1 + (3 - 1) = 3 cycles.
;
;     At 8 MHz:
;
;         3 x 125 ns = 375 ns.
;
;     Therefore, the GPIO remains LOW for 375 ns.
;
; WHY IS THIS INSTRUCTION LOW?
;
; Every WS2812 data bit must finish with a LOW period.
;
; This instruction provides the final 3 LOW cycles of the
; previous bit, while simultaneously reading the next bit.
;
; For a logical ZERO:
;
;     Previous NOP LOW = 5 cycles.
;     This OUT LOW     = 3 cycles.
;     Total LOW        = 8 cycles.
;
; For a logical ONE:
;
;     This OUT provides all 3 LOW cycles.
;
; IMPORTANT:
;
; If autopull is enabled and the TX FIFO is empty when new data
; is required, OUT can stall while waiting for data.
;
; During that stall, the output remains LOW, extending the LOW
; period. This is useful between LED frames, but the host must
; supply data fast enough to avoid unwanted gaps within a frame.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; INSTRUCTION 2: CHECK WHETHER THE BIT IS ZERO
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

    jmp !x, do_zero side 1 [T1 - 1]

; This instruction:
;
;     1. Checks the value stored in X.
;     2. Selects the ZERO or ONE execution path.
;     3. Drives the GPIO HIGH for 2 clock cycles.
;
; BREAKDOWN:
;
; "jmp"
;     JMP means JUMP.
;
;     It changes which instruction the PIO executes next.
;
;     A jump can be unconditional or depend on a condition.
;
; "!x"
;     This is a PIO jump condition meaning "X equals zero".
;
;     It is NOT a bitwise inversion of X.
;
;     If X == 0, the condition is TRUE.
;     If X != 0, the condition is FALSE.
;
; "do_zero"
;     This is the label of the instruction used for a ZERO bit.
;
;     If X == 0:
;         Jump to do_zero.
;
;     If X != 0:
;         Continue to the next instruction (do_one).
;
; "side 1"
;     Drive the GPIO HIGH.
;
;     IMPORTANT: The GPIO goes HIGH regardless of whether
;     the conditional jump is taken.
;
;     Both ZERO and ONE bits begin with a HIGH pulse.
;
; "[T1 - 1]"
;     T1 = 2.
;
;     Total cycles = 1 + (2 - 1) = 2 cycles.
;
;     2 x 125 ns = 250 ns.
;
;     Therefore, the GPIO remains HIGH for 250 ns.
;
; WHY DOES THIS MATTER?
;
; For logical ZERO:
;
;     The GPIO will go LOW after these 2 cycles.
;
; For logical ONE:
;
;     The next instruction keeps the GPIO HIGH for another
;     5 cycles, giving 7 HIGH cycles in total.
;
; This is how the program produces different pulse widths.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; LOGICAL ONE PATH
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

do_one:

; This label marks the instruction used to transmit a logical ONE.
;
; Execution reaches this instruction only when X is not zero.
;
; The previous conditional jump was NOT taken.
;
; Labels are not instructions and consume no clock cycles.


    jmp bitloop     side 1 [T2 - 1]

; This instruction:
;
;     1. Keeps the GPIO HIGH.
;     2. Waits for 5 clock cycles in total.
;     3. Jumps back to bitloop to process the next bit.
;
; BREAKDOWN:
;
; "jmp bitloop"
;     Unconditionally jump back to the instruction labelled bitloop.
;
;     Unlike "jmp !x", this jump has no condition.
;     It always jumps.
;
; "side 1"
;     Keep the side-set GPIO HIGH.
;
;     The previous instruction already set it HIGH.
;     Repeating side 1 ensures it remains HIGH.
;
; "[T2 - 1]"
;     T2 = 5.
;
;     Total cycles = 1 + (5 - 1) = 5 cycles.
;
;     5 x 125 ns = 625 ns.
;
; LOGICAL ONE TIMING:
;
;     Previous conditional JMP:
;         HIGH for T1 = 2 cycles.
;
;     This unconditional JMP:
;         HIGH for T2 = 5 cycles.
;
;     Total HIGH = T1 + T2
;                = 2 + 5
;                = 7 cycles
;                = 875 ns.
;
; The JMP returns execution to bitloop.
;
; The next OUT instruction sets the GPIO LOW for T3 = 3 cycles:
;
;     LOW = 3 x 125 ns = 375 ns.
;
; COMPLETE LOGICAL ONE:
;
;     HIGH = 7 cycles = 875 ns.
;     LOW  = 3 cycles = 375 ns.
;
;     Total = 10 cycles = 1250 ns.
;
; WHY JUMP BACK HERE?
;
; The logical ONE has finished its HIGH portion.
;
; Returning to bitloop begins the LOW portion and extracts
; the next data bit at the same time.
;
; The instruction below (do_zero) is skipped completely
; when transmitting a ONE.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; LOGICAL ZERO PATH
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

do_zero:

; This label marks the instruction used to transmit a logical ZERO.
;
; Execution reaches this instruction when:
;
;     X == 0
;
; The earlier conditional jump:
;
;     jmp !x, do_zero
;
; jumps directly here, skipping the logical ONE instruction.
;
; Labels do not consume any clock cycles.


    nop             side 0 [T2 - 1]

; This instruction:
;
;     1. Performs no data-processing operation.
;     2. Drives the GPIO LOW.
;     3. Keeps it LOW for 5 clock cycles.
;
; BREAKDOWN:
;
; "nop"
;     NOP means "No Operation".
;
;     It does not change the PIO data registers.
;
;     However, it is still a real instruction.
;     It takes at least one clock cycle to execute.
;
;     It can also perform side-set and include a delay.
;
; WHY USE NOP?
;
; We need the GPIO to remain LOW for a specific time,
; but there is no additional data-processing work to do.
;
; NOP provides the required timing without changing registers.
;
; "side 0"
;     Drive the side-set GPIO LOW.
;
;     The previous instruction set it HIGH for 2 cycles.
;
;     This transition from HIGH to LOW completes the short
;     HIGH pulse that represents a logical ZERO.
;
; "[T2 - 1]"
;     T2 = 5.
;
;     Total cycles = 1 + (5 - 1) = 5 cycles.
;
;     5 x 125 ns = 625 ns.
;
; WHAT HAPPENS NEXT?
;
; This NOP is the final instruction before ".wrap".
;
; The PIO hardware automatically returns to ".wrap_target",
; which is the OUT instruction at bitloop.
;
; That OUT instruction keeps the GPIO LOW for another 3 cycles.
;
; LOGICAL ZERO TIMING:
;
;     Conditional JMP:
;         HIGH for T1 = 2 cycles.
;
;     This NOP:
;         LOW for T2 = 5 cycles.
;
;     Next OUT:
;         LOW for T3 = 3 cycles.
;
;     Total HIGH = 2 cycles = 250 ns.
;     Total LOW  = 5 + 3 = 8 cycles = 1000 ns.
;
; COMPLETE LOGICAL ZERO:
;
;     HIGH = 2 cycles = 250 ns.
;     LOW  = 8 cycles = 1000 ns.
;
;     Total = 10 cycles = 1250 ns.
;
; WHY NOT USE "jmp bitloop" HERE?
;
; We could use a JMP instruction, but automatic wrapping
; already returns execution to bitloop.
;
; Using NOP makes the ZERO path simple and avoids an
; unnecessary explicit jump.
;
; Both NOP and JMP take one base clock cycle, so the timing
; is the same when they use the same delay value.


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; END OF THE AUTOMATIC REPEAT SECTION
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

.wrap

; ".wrap" tells the PIO assembler where the repeating section ends.
;
; When the preceding NOP finishes, the PIO automatically continues
; execution at the instruction marked by ".wrap_target".
;
; In this program:
;
;     .wrap_target -> bitloop -> OUT instruction.
;
; The automatic wrap does NOT require an additional clock cycle.
;
; The TWO possible execution paths are:
;
; LOGICAL ZERO (X == 0):
;
;     bitloop:
;         OUT, GPIO LOW (3 cycles)
;             |
;             v
;         JMP !X, do_zero, GPIO HIGH (2 cycles)
;             |
;             | X == 0, jump taken
;             v
;     do_zero:
;         NOP, GPIO LOW (5 cycles)
;             |
;             v
;         .wrap -> automatically return to bitloop
;
;
; LOGICAL ONE (X == 1):
;
;     bitloop:
;         OUT, GPIO LOW (3 cycles)
;             |
;             v
;         JMP !X, do_zero, GPIO HIGH (2 cycles)
;             |
;             | X != 0, jump NOT taken
;             v
;     do_one:
;         JMP bitloop, GPIO HIGH (5 cycles)
;             |
;             v
;         Return to bitloop
;
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; FINAL NOTES
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; 1. This program contains only FOUR executable PIO instructions.
;
; 2. Each transmitted bit takes exactly TEN clock cycles when
;    execution does not stall waiting for data.
;
; 3. Side-set controls the GPIO while the main instructions
;    read data, make decisions, and control execution flow.
;
; 4. The host program must configure:
;
;       - PIO clock frequency (8 MHz).
;       - Side-set GPIO pin and output direction.
;       - OUT shift direction (MSB first).
;       - FIFO/autopull behaviour.
;       - Data packing and transmission.
;
; 5. After sending a complete LED frame, the data line must
;    remain LOW for the WS2812 reset/latch interval.
;    Use the reset interval required by the LED's datasheet.
;
; 6. If the PIO stalls waiting for new TX data, the LOW interval
;    can become longer than the nominal timing.
;
; 7. The first OUT instruction also produces an initial LOW
;    interval before the first bit's HIGH pulse.
;
; 8. The output bit rate is:
;
;       PIO clock / cycles per bit
;       = 8,000,000 / 10
;       = 800,000 bits per second.
;
; =====================================================================
; END OF WS2812 PIO PROGRAM
; =====================================================================
